WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.143
Every year, the federal government buys

2
00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:12.523
almost $800 billion of goods and services.

3
00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:15.823
Every one of those dollars moves
through a contracting office.

4
00:00:17.371 --> 00:00:20.841
And those offices are being
asked to do more with less.

5
00:00:21.251 --> 00:00:25.761
Agency workforces are down
more than 11% since late 2024.

6
00:00:26.171 --> 00:00:27.761
The FAR itself is being rewritten.

7
00:00:27.791 --> 00:00:32.601
And for the biggest awards, lead times
have grown by more than two months.

8
00:00:32.945 --> 00:00:36.555
An acquisition moves through
four phases: the requirement,

9
00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:39.535
the solicitation, the
evaluation, and the award.

10
00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:42.015
In every one of them,
the work still comes down

11
00:00:42.045 --> 00:00:45.775
to people reading, re-keying,
and documenting by hand.

12
00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:51.420
Nowhere is that heavier
than in source selection.

13
00:00:51.605 --> 00:00:54.915
Evaluators read hundreds
of pages per proposal.

14
00:00:55.358 --> 00:00:58.968
Contracting officers check
compliance and prices line by line.

15
00:01:00.362 --> 00:01:03.272
Splits are reconciled
in hours of meetings,

16
00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:06.752
and the decision document is
written from scratch at the end.

17
00:01:08.146 --> 00:01:10.716
Every rating has to be
documented well enough

18
00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:14.456
to survive a protest, because
when protests are filed,

19
00:01:14.566 --> 00:01:17.496
more than half end with
relief for the protester.

20
00:01:18.154 --> 00:01:21.104
All told, the biggest
awards now take a median

21
00:01:21.134 --> 00:01:24.944
of 255 days from solicitation to award,

22
00:01:25.074 --> 00:01:28.604
much of it spent reading,
checking, and writing by hand.

23
00:01:29.552 --> 00:01:33.722
So let's look at source selection,
run end to end with UiPath.

24
00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:38.248
A source selection runs on six people:

25
00:01:38.278 --> 00:01:40.868
the contracting officer, three evaluators,

26
00:01:40.898 --> 00:01:43.348
the board chair, and the
source selection authority.

27
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:46.227
Meet Dana, a contracting officer.

28
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:48.887
She signs in to her source
selection workspace and sees

29
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:52.547
every evaluation she owns,
what's waiting on her, and what

30
00:01:52.577 --> 00:01:55.187
the robots and agents
are working on right now.

31
00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.330
Every evaluation shows its stage, and
anything waiting on her is one click away.

32
00:02:03.344 --> 00:02:05.534
A new requirement just
closed for proposals.

33
00:02:05.564 --> 00:02:11.220
Dana uploads the solicitation, the cost
estimate, and the evaluation criteria.

34
00:02:11.788 --> 00:02:15.578
UiPath agents read them and
build the evaluation plan:

35
00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:19.438
the factors and their weights, the
price lines, and the page limits.

36
00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:22.649
Nobody re-keys Section M.

37
00:02:23.465 --> 00:02:27.115
She assigns the evaluation
board: who rates which factor.

38
00:02:27.334 --> 00:02:31.224
Each offeror's volumes are matched
automatically from the file names.

39
00:02:32.586 --> 00:02:37.496
Dana adds the proposals, checks the
plan, and starts the source selection.

40
00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:43.802
Maestro starts the run, and the
robots begin ingesting the proposals.

41
00:02:44.484 --> 00:02:48.074
When proposals arrived,
IXP read every volume,

42
00:02:48.104 --> 00:02:50.274
and robots ran the
compliance and responsibility

43
00:02:50.304 --> 00:02:52.634
checks before anyone opened a page.

44
00:02:55.016 --> 00:02:59.486
Each offeror was screened against
SAM.gov, the exclusions list,

45
00:02:59.576 --> 00:03:03.586
and federal integrity records,
and IXP pulled the key facts

46
00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.126
from every cover letter
and price schedule.

47
00:03:06.281 --> 00:03:09.231
Two of the three evaluators
have already submitted.

48
00:03:09.649 --> 00:03:11.799
Tom is the last evaluator.

49
00:03:12.043 --> 00:03:14.053
Before he sees a single proposal,

50
00:03:14.083 --> 00:03:16.753
he certifies that he has
no conflict of interest.

51
00:03:17.983 --> 00:03:21.073
Instead of a blank worksheet,
he starts from findings

52
00:03:21.103 --> 00:03:25.333
an agent drafted, each tied to
the exact page it came from.

53
00:03:25.428 --> 00:03:30.258
He accepts some, rewrites one, and
rejects one that misses the point.

54
00:03:33.403 --> 00:03:36.313
The ratings are his. The agent drafts.

55
00:03:36.543 --> 00:03:39.693
Tom decides, and both
versions stay in the record.

56
00:03:42.793 --> 00:03:46.543
With all three in, a robot
compiles the evaluations,

57
00:03:46.713 --> 00:03:49.783
and an agent proposes a
consensus, showing exactly where

58
00:03:49.813 --> 00:03:53.523
the evaluators disagreed and
why it reconciled each split.

59
00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:58.443
Rebecca, the board chair,
sees each evaluator's

60
00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:01.203
rating and the findings
behind every split.

61
00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:05.023
She confirms each one and
signs the consensus report.

62
00:04:08.082 --> 00:04:10.352
Now back to Dana for price.

63
00:04:10.702 --> 00:04:12.312
Robots have already compared every offer

64
00:04:12.342 --> 00:04:13.992
and line item to the government estimate,

65
00:04:14.022 --> 00:04:18.152
and flagged the one total
that's more than 10% below it.

66
00:04:18.262 --> 00:04:20.232
Dana records how she's
treating that variance,

67
00:04:20.262 --> 00:04:22.632
and her price reasonableness determination

68
00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:25.952
drafts itself from her
choices, citing the FAR.

69
00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:30.071
Finally, we can switch to James,
who's the source selection authority.

70
00:04:30.281 --> 00:04:32.211
An agent has drafted the decision document

71
00:04:32.241 --> 00:04:35.471
from the consensus record,
but the decision is his.

72
00:04:35.529 --> 00:04:37.939
He writes his rationale and signs.

73
00:04:38.245 --> 00:04:41.535
The complete award record
is filed automatically.

74
00:04:41.822 --> 00:04:44.572
Release, consensus, price, and award:

75
00:04:44.602 --> 00:04:48.032
every decision is in the record,
with who made it and when.

76
00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:50.884
And the award doesn't stop there.

77
00:04:51.034 --> 00:04:53.584
The record opens straight
into Contract Management,

78
00:04:53.614 --> 00:04:56.364
where the award phase
picks up: the contract,

79
00:04:56.394 --> 00:04:58.384
the notices, and the debriefings.

80
00:04:59.878 --> 00:05:02.308
Behind it all, one Maestro process ran

81
00:05:02.338 --> 00:05:05.248
the whole source
selection: robots, agents,

82
00:05:05.278 --> 00:05:08.348
and people, each doing
the part they're best at.

83
00:05:10.092 --> 00:05:14.482
And if a protest comes, every rating
traces back to the evaluators,

84
00:05:14.512 --> 00:05:17.962
the findings, and the exact
proposal pages behind it.

85
00:05:20.606 --> 00:05:24.656
In this evaluation, that meant
every proposal page read,

86
00:05:24.746 --> 00:05:26.936
every finding drafted with a citation,

87
00:05:27.046 --> 00:05:30.436
and a protest-ready record
built as the work happened.

88
00:05:32.743 --> 00:05:36.933
It's all built on the UiPath
platform: Maestro to orchestrate,

89
00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:40.333
agents and IXP to read and
draft, robots to check,

90
00:05:40.543 --> 00:05:43.893
Data Fabric for the record, and
Action Center for every decision.

91
00:05:44.218 --> 00:05:47.528
In this run, robots and
agents did all the reading,

92
00:05:47.558 --> 00:05:50.288
checking and drafting
in under six minutes.

93
00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:53.888
Every hour the team spent went
to judgment, not paperwork.

94
00:05:54.318 --> 00:05:56.508
Source selection is where we start.

95
00:05:56.578 --> 00:06:01.028
The same approach runs across the whole
acquisition, from requirement to award.

96
00:06:01.701 --> 00:06:05.216
Robots do. Agents think. People lead.
